#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n1. $(1,-5)$\n2. $(4,-1)$\n3. $(-5,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ$. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRX &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5)$, and $(4,-3)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(-2,1)$\n2. $(-4,3)$\n3. $(-5,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/38d3b5868bf5043351514f3dff7dd033.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= Ai = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = j \\\\\nj^{′} &= Aj = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} =-i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/2d169b5fa006402034a1f405a9c1cb45.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v}$, we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/12a8c4243623a4dea1baaca392b2af92.png)"}]}
